DerivedData/
*.xcodeproj
xcuserdata/
.DS_Store
benchmark/.venv/

# ollama pull transcript (ANSI progress bars, regenerated by pull_models.sh)
benchmark/pull.log

# Real dictation fixtures sampled from ~/Documents/superwhisper/recordings/.
# Louis's own work content (clients, internal material): usable locally for
# fixtures and tests, NEVER committed. Decision recorded as ruling R12.
benchmark/*.local.jsonl
benchmark/results-*.local.jsonl
benchmark/__pycache__/

# Benchmark v2 generation log: results-v2.jsonl carries 8 rows generated on
# `real_long` fixtures, i.e. Louis's own dictated work content. Same rule as
# *.local.jsonl (ruling R12): usable locally, never committed. The JUDGED
# evidence -- packets_v2/, judgments_v2/, letter_mapping_v2.json -- is 100 %
# synthetic (verified: zero `r-verylong-*` references) and IS committed, so the
# 765 blind scores stay reproducible without shipping his content.
benchmark/results-v2.jsonl
benchmark/run_v2.log
benchmark/judges_v2.log

# Benchmark v3 generation outputs. Same rule as v2 (ruling R12): the `real`
# fixture set is Louis's own dictated work content (clients, internal material),
# usable locally, NEVER committed. `results-v3.jsonl` carries it, so it is
# ignored wholesale rather than split.
benchmark/results-v3.jsonl
# `results-v3-synthetic.jsonl` is the exception and is committed: it is the
# 150-row prompt arm on `fixtures.jsonl`, which is 100 % synthetic. It is what
# the blind judges are given, so it has to stay reproducible.
benchmark/run_v3*.log

# NOT ignored, deliberately: benchmark/ram-v3.json, ram-v3-ctxsweep.json and
# ram-v3-cold6.json are pure machine measurements (RSS, `ollama ps`, latencies).
# They contain no dictation text, and they are the evidence behind the v3
# admissibility table, so they belong in the repo.

# Agent worktrees -- transient, created by the harness, never part of the project.
.claude/

# Two tools fight over this file, so neither state is committable. Measured in a fresh clone:
# `xcodebuild` writes FOUR pins into it (dynamicnotchkit, whisperkit, swift-argument-parser and
# grdb.swift -- it resolves the whole app graph and, with the .xcodeproj gitignored, has nowhere
# else to put it), and `swift build` immediately rewrites it back to the ONE that MurmureCore
# actually depends on. Committing either state guarantees a dirty tree after the other tool runs,
# which is what a fresh clone reported on its first build.
#
# What is lost by ignoring it: the resolved file also pinned the git revision behind GRDB's tag,
# and a tag can be moved. What replaces it: `Package.swift` pins GRDB with `exact:`, which no tool
# rewrites -- so the version is fixed in the manifest, where it is readable, and only the exact
# commit behind an unchanged tag is unguarded.
MurmureCore/Package.resolved
